---
title: Irish soda bread
servings: 8
yield: 1 loaf, 9 inches
tags: [irish, vegetarian, quick]
cuisine: irish
time: 1 hour 5 minutes
source: Irish Soda Bread by King Arthur Baking, https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/recipes/irish-soda-bread-recipe
---

Heat the oven to 400°F (205°C). Lightly grease an #8- or 9-inch round cake pan{}.

In a #large bowl{}, whisk the @Irish-style flour{276%g}, @bread flour{150%g}, @granulated sugar{37%g}, @baking soda{1%tsp}, @table salt{3/4%tsp} and @dried currants{95%g}(or 99 g raisins).

Rub in the @unsalted butter{57%g}(cold, in 8 pieces) until no big lumps are left.

Whisk the @buttermilk{302%g} with the @eggs{1} and stir it into the dry mix. The dough is stiff; add 1 or 2 tablespoons more buttermilk if it stays crumbly.

Knead it briefly, shape it into a ball, flatten it slightly and set it in the pan. Cut a cross 1/2 inch deep across the top.

Bake ~{45%minutes} to 55 minutes, until golden and a tester comes out clean. Brush the top with @unsalted butter{28%g}(melted, optional).

Cut it with a serrated knife.

> Irish-style flour is a soft wheat flour with some bran in it. Without it, use
> 2 1/4 cups whole wheat flour and all-purpose flour in place of the bread flour.
